# Initial Prompt

We are competing in the Tech for Change 2026 hackathon.
Theme:
"Tech for Change brings UQ students together to design practical, socially responsible ICT solutions for real-world regional challenges. Through a one-day bootcamp, mentoring, teamwork, and final pitches, students build employability skills while creating ideas for meaningful social impact."
Team:

•  2 Engineering/Mathematics students
•  1 Computer Science/Mathematics student
•  2 Engineering/Master of Engineering students
  Time available:
•  8 hours to design, build an MVP, and prepare the final presentation.
  Judging Criteria:
•  Problem Definition (supported by evidence): 20%
•  Strategy/Solution: 50%
•  What Does Success Look Like?: 10%
•  Delivery and Presentation: 20%
  Generate 15 hackathon ideas that are highly likely to score well against this rubric.
  For each idea, provide:

1. Project Name

2. Problem Definition (20%)

•  Define the regional problem.
•  Include supporting evidence, statistics, or trends where possible.
•  Explain why the problem is important.

3. Strategy / Solution (50%)

•  Explain the ICT solution in detail.
•  Describe the user journey.
•  Explain why this solution is better than existing approaches.
•  List the core technologies (AI, GIS, computer vision, optimisation, IoT, etc.).
•  Clearly define what can realistically be built in an 8-hour MVP.

4. Success Metrics (10%)

Provide measurable KPIs such as:

•  Time saved
•  Cost reduction
•  Increased accessibility
•  Number of users helped
•  Accuracy improvements
•  Environmental impact
•  Other relevant metrics

5. Presentation Potential (20%)

•  Why this project would make an impressive live demo.
•  What judges would see in a 2–3 minute pitch.
•  What "wow factor" the project has.

6. Difficulty (1–10)

7. Innovation (1–10)

8. Social Impact (1–10)

9. Feasibility in 8 Hours (1–10)

Finally:

•  Rank all ideas from strongest to weakest.
•  Recommend the best five.
•  Explain how each recommended idea scores against each judging criterion.
•  Prioritise ideas that are unique, memorable, technically feasible in one day, and capable of impressing judges during a live demonstration rather than ideas that require extensive backend infrastructure.
